Good instinct to question the qualitative basis of the score — it's a common tension in TM. Here's how to get more signal from the data you already have.

---

## The core problem with raw country risk scores

A single ordinal score (e.g. 1–10) used directly as a feature has two weaknesses:

- **Non-linearity is ignored** — the difference between a score of 7 and 8 may not be the same as between 3 and 4
- **Context collapse** — a score of 6 for a customer's nationality vs. a score of 6 for a transaction destination aren't equivalent signals

---

## Feature engineering ideas, grouped by approach

### 1. Decompose the score into its components
Rather than using the composite score, try to source its sub-components separately — FATF status, Basel AML index sub-scores, Transparency International CPI, tax haven lists, sanctions exposure. Each becomes its own feature, and you let the model learn which dimensions actually predict risk in *your* portfolio.

### 2. Interaction features across dimensions
You're already looking at nationality and transaction flows separately — now combine them:
- **Nationality × destination mismatch** — customer from low-risk country sending to high-risk destination (or vice versa) is often more informative than either alone
- **Origin × destination pair score delta** — the *difference* in risk scores between sending and receiving country, not just their individual values
- **Triple interaction** — nationality / origin / destination all high risk simultaneously as a binary flag

### 3. Behavioural features over time
The ratio features you have are a good start. Extend them with:
- **Rolling high-risk country exposure** — % of transaction volume to/from high-risk countries over 30/60/90 day windows (trend matters)
- **Entropy of destination countries** — customers with highly concentrated flows to one high-risk country vs. dispersed flows tell different stories
- **Shift detection** — did the customer's country risk profile change recently? A new high-risk destination appearing in the last 30 days is more suspicious than a stable historical pattern

### 4. Network / peer features
- **Counterparty country risk** — if you can see who the customer transacts *with*, what is the average country risk score of their counterparty network?
- **Peer group deviation** — how does this customer's high-risk country ratio compare to similar customers (same segment, product, geography)?

### 5. Replace the score with a learned embedding
If you have labelled data (SARs, confirmed cases), you can treat country as a categorical and learn an embedding — the model implicitly creates a richer representation than any qualitative score. Country2Vec-style approaches or simple entity embeddings in a gradient boosted model work well here.

### 6. Calibrate the score against your own data
Run a calibration analysis: for each score band, what is the SAR rate in your portfolio? You may find the qualitative score has poor monotonicity in your data — some "high risk" countries produce few alerts, some "medium" countries produce many. Use this to build a **portfolio-calibrated risk score** as a feature alongside the original.

---

## Priority order if you're starting now

| Priority | Feature | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Nationality × destination mismatch flag | High signal, low cost |
| 2 | Rolling 30/60/90d high-risk volume % | Adds temporal dimension |
| 3 | Destination country entropy | Catches structuring across borders |
| 4 | Score delta (origin vs destination) | Better than raw scores |
| 5 | Portfolio-calibrated score | Fixes the qualitative problem directly |

The calibration analysis (priority 5) is also worth doing early — it will tell you how much to trust the underlying score at all, which shapes everything else.
